Android codegen for `$"..."` translatable strings. A package that uses i18n
gets the Kotlin runtime injected: I18n.kt under the module's kotlin/ tree,
`import us.duckfam.git.jonathan.sngl.i18n.I18n` in MainScreen.kt, and
`I18n.init(this)` in MainActivity.onCreate.

Nobody writes the locale. After NoContext, a locale read is lowered to a
hidden state var on the component -- `var __ctx_locale by remember {
mutableStateOf(I18n.defaultLocale()) }` -- and threaded into every per-locale
call as its first argument.

The string here takes an argument and is recomputed in the click handler, so
the plural template and its `mapOf("count" to count)` come out twice, once in
the initializer and once in the assignment. The minimal case -- one
argument-free string, no handler -- is generate_android_i18n_app_with_manifest.txtar.

i18n.manifest.json in the package root is copied verbatim to
app/src/main/assets/, where the runtime loads it.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:i18n"
import app "sngl:app"

output { kotlin { android(main=true) } }

component main node {
    var count int = 1
    var label = $"You have {count, plural, one{# item} other{# items}}"
    text(value=label)
    button(text="Add", @click {
        count = count + 1
        label = $"You have {count, plural, one{# item} other{# items}}"
    })
}

app.window {
    main
}
-- i18n.manifest.json --
{"Hello":{"en":"Hello","fr":"Bonjour"}}
